---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Header from '../../../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../../../components/Footer.astro';
import ArticleNavigation from '../../../components/ArticleNavigation.astro';

const articleNavigation = [
  { href: '#problem', label: '01 / Where coupling starts' },
  { href: '#boundary', label: '02 / The architectural boundary' },
  { href: '#rules', label: '03 / Making behavior explicit' },
  { href: '#failures', label: '04 / Failure paths' },
  { href: '#testing', label: '05 / How to test it' },
  { href: '#tradeoffs', label: '06 / What the design costs' },
  { href: '#reflection', label: '07 / What I learned' },
] as const;

const title = 'Redesigning Fleet Interactions — Priyansh Sharma';
const description =
  'An engineering account of the October 2026 Fleet interaction framework redesign within Order Scheduler, with conceptual illustrations of planning, execution, and integration boundaries.';
const cases = [
  {
    id: '01',
    title: 'An order is not eligible yet',
    question: 'What if eligibility changes between planning and execution?',
    answer:
      'Planning selects an interaction from supplied context. A valid selection can become invalid before execution. Checking necessary preconditions at the relevant action boundary is distinct from re-evaluating all selection policy inside the executor; side-effect-free planning alone does not keep inputs fresh.',
    cost: 'Preconditions need explicit semantics. Proposed check: test an initially ineligible order, then an eligible decision whose preconditions change before execution. Under the illustrative policy, assert that neither permits a prohibited provider call. This does not prescribe a production rejection or replanning protocol.',
  },
  {
    id: '02',
    title: 'A provider call times out',
    question: 'Was the operation executed remotely?',
    answer:
      'A missing response leaves several possibilities: failure before acceptance, success with a lost response, or an outcome still unknown to the scheduler. Safe retry or reconciliation depends on the provider contract. Status queries and idempotency support cannot be assumed, and a centralized boundary alone cannot prevent duplicate external effects.',
    cost: 'Uncertainty may delay progress. Proposed check: simulate acceptance with a lost response separately from a known rejection. Assert that the unknown outcome is not recorded as confirmed failure and that any retry or reconciliation follows the simulated provider contract.',
  },
  {
    id: '03',
    title: 'Several order types are available',
    question: 'Who decides which one takes precedence?',
    answer:
      'The planner should resolve competing Fleet interactions using explicit precedence, tie, and fallback policies. Repeatable decisions require controlled state, configuration, and any time-dependent inputs. This concerns interaction selection, not the separate Rule Flow initiative.',
    cost: 'Each policy choice adds cases to maintain. Proposed check: supply competing eligible actions, a tie, and a no-match case with fixed inputs. Assert the selection prescribed by each illustrative policy, including no action where that policy requires it, without inventing production precedence rules.',
  },
  {
    id: '04',
    title: 'A result arrives after a retry',
    question: 'Can late, repeated, or concurrent results change the logical outcome incorrectly?',
    answer:
      'Correlate the result with the logical operation and the attempt it describes, then enforce the permitted state transition. Correlation or a separate state check is insufficient if competing handlers can both accept a result. A late observation must not overwrite a newer authoritative state merely because its operation identity matches.',
    cost: 'Acceptance rules must cover races as well as sequential delivery. Proposed check: replay a result, deliver duplicates concurrently, and deliver an older attempt after completion. Assert one application of the defined logical transition and no invalid state regression; this does not establish how many times the provider acted.',
  },
];
const matrix = [
  [
    'Eligible decision; preconditions change before execution',
    'Select an action from fixed planning inputs',
    'Check required execution preconditions without repeating selection policy',
    'No provider call prohibited by the illustrative preconditions',
  ],
  [
    'No policy matches the controlled inputs',
    'Select the fallback specified by the illustrative policy',
    'Execute only the resulting action, if any',
    'Observed action matches the fallback; no provider call for a no-action decision',
  ],
  [
    'Simulated retryable failure persists until the budget is exhausted',
    'Keep the intended interaction distinct from retry classification',
    'Adapter reports the outcome; the designated retry owner applies the budget',
    'Attempts stop at the configured limit or deadline; exhaustion is observable',
  ],
  [
    'Duplicate results race; an older attempt arrives later',
    'Keep the logical operation distinct from its attempts',
    'Correlate results and enforce valid transition acceptance',
    'One application of the defined logical transition; no invalid state regression',
  ],
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <Header />
  <main id="main" class="case-page case-v23 scheduler-study">
    <div class="shell case-hero">
      <a class="case-back" href="/#work">
        ← Back to selected work
      </a>
      <div class="case-meta">
        <span>ENGINEERING STORY / 02</span>
        <span>ORCHESTRATION · BACKEND ARCHITECTURE · RELIABILITY</span>
      </div>
      <h1>
        Redesigning Fleet <em>interactions.</em>
      </h1>
      <p class="case-standfirst">
        In October 2026, I designed and implemented the Fleet interaction framework within the
        existing Order Scheduler. I separated planning from execution and brought Fleet calls behind
        a centralized interaction boundary to make their control flow easier to reason about.
      </p>
      <div class="case-byline">
        <span>By Priyansh Sharma</span>
        <span>Professional engineering experience · Thales</span>
        <span>Conceptual architectural walkthrough</span>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="shell case-layout">
      <ArticleNavigation items={articleNavigation} />
      <article class="case-prose">
        <div class="case-disclosure">
          <strong>About this write-up.</strong> My responsibility was the framework's technical
          architecture and implementation; the upstream business and configuration specification was
          developed collaboratively. The scheduler already existed. Earlier legacy refactoring, Rule
          Flow and conditional order chaining, and later Fleet configuration simplification are
          separate initiatives. Diagrams, the illustrative contract, and hypothetical failure
          scenarios explain the reasoning without reproducing private production implementation
          details or claiming every example was implemented or tested. Disclosure review remains
          outstanding.
        </div>
        <section id="problem" class="case-section">
          <p class="case-label">01 / WHERE COUPLING STARTS</p>
          <h2>A scheduler can quietly become the whole system.</h2>
          <p>
            Fleet interactions were distributed across several legacy execution paths, with
            configuration decisions intertwined with the code performing the calls. Understanding a
            change meant tracing both the decision and its effects across those paths. That made it
            harder to assess how an internal change might affect established scheduler behavior.
          </p>
          <p>
            I designed the framework around a shared Fleet interaction boundary and separated two
            responsibilities within it: <em>which interaction should be selected?</em> and{' '}
            <em>how should that interaction be carried out?</em> Centralizing that boundary gave
            interaction ownership and control flow a consistent place in the design.
          </p>
          <div class="case-highlight">
            <span>THE DESIGN QUESTION</span>
            <strong>
              Can a scheduling decision be understood and tested without running the external
              operation it chooses?
            </strong>
          </div>
          <p>
            I implemented the redesign in stages, with compatibility guiding the migration of
            existing call paths. The constraint was to preserve established scheduler and
            provider-facing behavior while changing internal responsibilities. A clearer structure
            would not justify silently changing what those paths did.
          </p>
        </section>
        <section id="boundary" class="case-section">
          <p class="case-label">02 / THE ARCHITECTURAL BOUNDARY</p>
          <h2>Decide first. Execute second. Make the handoff obvious.</h2>
          <p>
            I made planning side-effect-free: the planner selects the intended Fleet interaction
            from supplied state and policy context. The executor carries out the selected operation
            through the integration boundary, where provider-specific interaction concerns belong.
            Results feed subsequent orchestration decisions. This separation lets decision logic be
            reasoned about and tested without performing the external operation.
          </p>
          <figure class="scheduler-figure">
            <div class="scheduler-figure-top">
              <span>FIG. 01 / RESPONSIBILITY BOUNDARIES</span>
              <span>CONCEPTUAL MODEL</span>
            </div>
            <div
              class="scheduler-flow"
              role="img"
              aria-label="Relevant state and rules feed a planner. The planner emits an action description to an executor. The executor uses a provider adapter and reports a result back to durable progress state."
            >
              <div class="scheduler-input">
                <span>CONTEXT</span>
                <strong>State + rules</strong>
                <small>Eligibility · precedence · fallback</small>
              </div>
              <div class="scheduler-flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">
                →
              </div>
              <div class="scheduler-node scheduler-key">
                <span>DECIDE</span>
                <strong>Planner</strong>
                <small>Choose an action</small>
              </div>
              <div class="scheduler-flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">
                →
              </div>
              <div class="scheduler-node">
                <span>DO</span>
                <strong>Executor</strong>
                <small>Run the chosen action</small>
              </div>
              <div class="scheduler-flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">
                →
              </div>
              <div class="scheduler-node">
                <span>INTEGRATE</span>
                <strong>Provider adapter</strong>
                <small>Translate the external contract</small>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="scheduler-output">
              <span>OUTCOME LOOP</span>
              <strong>Result + progress</strong>
              <p>
                Correlate the result, update durable state, and inform the next planning decision.
              </p>
            </div>
            <figcaption>
              An educational decomposition of responsibilities; it does not describe internal
              services, deployment topology, or proprietary protocols.
            </figcaption>
          </figure>
          <p>
            The <strong>decision contract</strong> makes the handoff explicit: an intended action
            and the context needed to execute it, without making the executor reconstruct the
            selection policy. Supplied inputs can become stale before execution. Side-effect-free
            planning does not make the whole scheduler independent of external state or establish
            that a decision remains valid indefinitely.
          </p>
          <div class="scheduler-contract" aria-label="Illustrative planning contract">
            <div>
              <span>ILLUSTRATIVE CONTRACT</span>
              <span>NOT PRODUCTION CODE</span>
            </div>
            <pre><code>{`Decision {
  operationId
  actionType
  targetReference
  policyContext
}`}</code></pre>
          </div>
          <p>
            This is an illustration, not a private API. An operationId identifies a logical
            operation; retries are separate attempts whose outcomes need attribution. Identity
            supports correlation, but does not itself enforce idempotency, safe concurrent result
            acceptance, or exactly-once provider effects.
          </p>
        </section>
        <section id="rules" class="case-section">
          <p class="case-label">03 / EXPLICIT BEHAVIOR</p>
          <h2>Rules are part of the architecture, not an afterthought.</h2>
          <p>
            Here, rules mean the decision policies governing Fleet interactions: precedence,
            fallback, result handling, and configuration. These are concerns of the framework
            boundary; the separate Rule Flow and conditional order-chaining initiative is outside
            this account. The cards below summarize conceptual questions, not specific policy
            semantics or a claim that each behavior changed in this redesign.
          </p>
          <p>
            One useful habit from this work was to write down the expected behavior before changing
            the framework. It turns “what happens here?” from an argument spread across conditionals
            into something reviewers and testers can inspect.
          </p>
          <div class="scheduler-rule-grid">
            <div>
              <span>01 / PRIORITY</span>
              <strong>What takes precedence?</strong>
              <p>Define how competing eligible actions are selected.</p>
            </div>
            <div>
              <span>02 / FALLBACK</span>
              <strong>What happens on no match?</strong>
              <p>Make the default path an explicit behavior.</p>
            </div>
            <div>
              <span>03 / RESULTS</span>
              <strong>Where does the outcome go?</strong>
              <p>Specify correlation and result publication.</p>
            </div>
            <div>
              <span>04 / FLAGS</span>
              <strong>What varies by configuration?</strong>
              <p>Keep policy switches distinct from execution plumbing.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>
        <section id="failures" class="case-section">
          <p class="case-label">04 / FAILURE PATHS</p>
          <h2>The interesting part happens when the happy path stops.</h2>
          <p>
            These proposed checks examine the Fleet boundary under changing state and uncertain
            outcomes. Retry responsibility must identify who classifies errors, who schedules
            another attempt, and who enforces an attempt limit or time budget and records
            exhaustion. Planning, execution, and integration must not each add an independent retry
            loop that defeats the intended bound. Persistent provider failures also require
            considering total in-flight work and retry pressure, not just the limit for one
            operation.
          </p>
          <div class="v23-scenarios">
            {cases.map((item) => (
              <details class="v23-scenario">
                <summary>
                  <span class="v23-number">{item.id}</span>
                  <span>{item.title}</span>
                  <span class="v23-plus" aria-hidden="true">
                    +
                  </span>
                </summary>
                <div class="scheduler-failure-detail">
                  <div>
                    <small>THE QUESTION</small>
                    <strong>{item.question}</strong>
                  </div>
                  <div>
                    <small>DESIGN RESPONSE</small>
                    <p>{item.answer}</p>
                  </div>
                  <div>
                    <small>TRADEOFF / PROPOSED CHECK</small>
                    <p>{item.cost}</p>
                  </div>
                </div>
              </details>
            ))}
          </div>
          <p class="scheduler-fineprint">
            These are hypothetical scenarios and proposed assertions, not production incidents,
            completed tests, or evidence of particular enforcement mechanisms. Bounded retries limit
            repeated attempts; they do not guarantee recovery. Permanent failures and exhausted
            budgets need explicit outcomes rather than indefinite retries.
          </p>
        </section>
        <section id="testing" class="case-section">
          <p class="case-label">05 / VALIDATION</p>
          <h2>Test the behavior, not just the method.</h2>
          <p>
            I would check selection, external execution, and result acceptance separately, then
            verify that they agree. The matrix defines controlled situations and observable
            criteria; it does not report executed regression tests. Any claim of preserved behavior
            would need actual results against an agreed baseline.
          </p>
          <div
            class="scheduler-matrix-scroll"
            role="region"
            aria-label="Order scheduler test matrix — scrollable table"
            tabindex="0"
          >
            <table class="scheduler-matrix">
              <caption>
                Illustrative behavioral test matrix — examples to verify, not a claim of completed
                tests.
              </caption>
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="col">Situation</th>
                  <th scope="col">Planning expectation</th>
                  <th scope="col">Execution expectation</th>
                  <th scope="col">Correctness property</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                {matrix.map((row) => (
                  <tr>
                    {row.map((cell, index) =>
                      index === 0 ? <th scope="row">{cell}</th> : <td>{cell}</td>,
                    )}
                  </tr>
                ))}
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p>
            Policy checks need fixed state, configuration, and relevant time inputs. Provider
            simulations should distinguish known rejection from acceptance with a lost response;
            retryability must follow the supported contract rather than treating every error alike.
            Result checks need concurrent delivery and interruption cases as well as sequential
            duplicates. Their logical-transition assertions do not establish exactly-once external
            effects or atomic updates across the scheduler and a provider.
          </p>
        </section>
        <section id="tradeoffs" class="case-section">
          <p class="case-label">06 / TRADEOFFS</p>
          <h2>Modularity doesn't make complexity disappear.</h2>
          <p>
            Separating planning and execution introduces a contract to maintain, more explicit state
            transitions, and the need to decide which layer owns retries and result handling. If
            those responsibilities are vague, the split can simply move the coupling somewhere else.
          </p>
          <div class="scheduler-tradeoffs">
            <div>
              <span>WHAT IMPROVES</span>
              <strong>Decisions can be reasoned about independently.</strong>
              <p>
                Policy can change without rewriting every provider call, and provider integrations
                can evolve without reimplementing scheduling decisions.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div>
              <span>WHAT IT COSTS</span>
              <strong>Boundaries need discipline.</strong>
              <p>
                Operation identity, error classification, progress semantics, and contracts become
                first-class design concerns that require documentation and testing.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>
        <section id="reflection" class="case-section case-final-section">
          <p class="case-label">07 / WHAT I TOOK AWAY</p>
          <h2>A good framework makes decisions easier to explain.</h2>
          <p>
            The part I value most from this kind of refactor isn't a new pattern or a cleaner class
            diagram. It's being able to ask why a particular action was selected, trace which
            component owns it, and describe what happens when execution doesn't go as planned.
          </p>
          <p>
            There's still no architecture that removes every exception. But a clearer boundary lets
            the exceptions live where they belong—and makes the next change less frightening.
          </p>
          <div class="case-highlight">
            <span>THE TAKEAWAY</span>
            <strong>
              The best abstraction is the one that makes behavior more predictable for the next
              engineer reading the code.
            </strong>
          </div>
          <p class="scheduler-fineprint">
            This case study is intended for architectural discussion only. All examples require
            personal accuracy and employer-confidentiality review before public publication.
          </p>
        </section>
        <div class="v23-story-end">
          <div>
            <span>PREVIOUS ENGINEERING STORY</span>
            <strong>Distributed billing and reporting</strong>
          </div>
          <a href="/work/distributed-billing/">Read the case study ↗</a>
        </div>
      </article>
    </div>
  </main>
  <Footer credit="Designed and built with care." brandLabel="Back to homepage" />
</BaseLayout>
